(LST-32 — printing a list is recursive and cycle-safe: a list that contains
 itself is capped at a depth of 64, the over-deep subtree prints as `...`,
 the error flag is set, and printing unwinds safely instead of overflowing
 the stack. Hand-counted: exactly 64 opening brackets and 64 closing ones.
 Also covers: LST-33 (the worked example of LANGUAGE.md:2363-2366 — note
 that its inline comment "(prints: [[...]] then cyclic)" is an abbreviation,
 not a transcript; see Discrepancy 6).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST-32.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   [[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[...]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]
   cyclic
)
a list called ring is [].
append ring to ring.
print ring.
on error print "cyclic".
